Connect accumulated change and local rate under stated conditions (@math-fundamental-theorem: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Investigate-a-changing-rate-with-accumulation-and-approximation-bounds.lingua#r_0580A36881B6386A628584D117") { r_0005E569C642147DA7E568DFE0
Let f be continuous on [a,b], and define A(x)=∫_a^x f(t) dt. The integral exists by the stated continuity theorem. For an interior x and nonzero h with x+h in the interval, additivity gives [A(x+h)−A(x)]/h=(1/h)∫_x^(x+h)f(t) dt. This is the average value over the short interval; for h<0 both orientation and denominator reverse. Subtract f(x). Continuity makes |f(t)−f(x)| smaller than any chosen ε throughout a sufficiently short interval, so integral comparison bounds the quotient's difference from f(x) by ε. Hence A′(x)=f(x). This is the first fundamental connection between accumulation and rate.

An antiderivative F of f on an interval is a differentiable function with F′=f there. It is a function, not a single accumulated number. Suppose F is continuous on [a,b] and F′=f on (a,b), with f continuous on [a,b]. The function F−A has zero derivative on the interior. The mean value theorem makes it constant, with endpoint continuity extending the equality to a and b. Since A(a)=0, A(b)=F(b)−F(a). Thus ∫_a^b f=F(b)−F(a). Adding a constant to F changes neither its derivative nor this endpoint difference.

For f(s)=s²−1, the polynomial derivative rule gives F(s)=s³/3−s as an antiderivative. Evaluation on [0,2] gives 2/3, agreeing with the independently derived partition limit. On [0,1] it gives −2/3, and on [1,2] it gives 4/3. A volume with initial value 5 L therefore has dimensionless coordinate V(s)=5+s³/3−s. Its rate, initial value and accumulation agree under the stated model. A rate alone leaves the additive initial constant undetermined.

Continuity is a sufficient hypothesis in this version of the theorem; do not erase it when interpreting the conclusion. The step j from the preceding lesson is integrable but its accumulation has no derivative at the jump. In fact, no function can have j as its derivative on a whole interval around the jump: on the left any proposed antiderivative must be constant by the mean value theorem, and on the right it must have slope 2. Continuity at the join would then force unequal one-sided derivatives. This elementary example explains why a broad prose claim that every integrable function has an antiderivative needs qualification.

**Read critically:** [Calculus and differential-equation preparation — Strang and Herman, OpenStax](../../chemistry/industrial-chemistry/sources/Calculus-and-Differential-Equations-OpenStax.lingua#r_027WKVM5NG5X84M86TSVRA77J3), Volume 1 section 5.3, both theorem statements and proofs. The theorem states continuity explicitly; retain it even where accompanying prose speaks more broadly. Explain the negative-h case of the average-value argument. Compare the polynomial's sum-based and derivative-based calculations without using one as a premise for proving the other.

**Transfer:** explain why the area under a velocity curve can give displacement while the absolute area gives distance for one-dimensional motion. Connect with [Investigate a cart with motion, momentum and energy](../../physics/core/Investigate-a-cart-with-motion-momentum-and-energy.lingua#r_005C89AACDDC8AD7217B4BF754) without changing its original piecewise model. A thermal energy balance can integrate a time-dependent transfer rate once quantities and boundary are defined; the theorem does not establish the physical rate law. General differential equations, multivariable integrals and discontinuous/impulsive models require later formation.

**Continue:** [Investigate a changing rate with accumulation and approximation bounds](Investigate-a-changing-rate-with-accumulation-and-approximation-bounds.lingua#r_0580A36881B6386A628584D117).

